WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.380
Not every leak is a missing cleanup call.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.040
Some leaks are logical retention problems.

3
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:17.580
A long-running service may keep objects in maps, queues, caches, registries, telemetry buffers, session tables, or retry state long after those objects are useful.

4
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:22.100
Caches need size limits and eviction policies.

5
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:29.560
A cache that grows forever is not behaving like a cache; it is behaving like unbounded retained state.

6
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:36.860
Policies should define maximum entries, maximum memory, entry age, invalidation triggers, and cleanup at shutdown.

7
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.220
Queues and buffers need backpressure.

8
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:45.240
If work arrives faster than it can be processed, retained work items can consume memory indefinitely.

9
00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:55.260
A robust design defines queue limits, rejection behavior, priority handling, retry rules, and operator-visible signals when pressure rises.

10
00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.420
Request and session state should expire predictably.

11
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:06.040
Connection registries, user sessions, per-request context, and temporary records should have clear lifecycle boundaries.

12
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.300
Cleanup should not depend on rare shutdown events or manual intervention.

13
00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:14.020
Monitoring helps detect unexpected resource growth.

14
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:25.400
Metrics for memory use, queue depth, cache size, object counts, handle counts, and cleanup failures can reveal a retention problem before it becomes an outage.

15
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.220
Operational visibility is part of leak prevention.

16
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.620
Operational limits should be treated as design requirements, not afterthoughts.

17
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:42.440
A system should define what happens at capacity, how old state ages out, and who is alerted when retained state grows unexpectedly.
